# Caddy in front of private-stock (§12).
#
# Deliberately a *whole* Caddyfile rather than a `conf.d` fragment like the sibling Pi apps
# use, because this one runs in its own container beside the app rather than on a host that
# already has a shared Caddy. If you instead front this from the Pi's existing Caddy, the site
# block below is still the thing to copy into `/etc/caddy/conf.d/private-stock.caddy` — change
# `reverse_proxy` to name the NAS (`reverse_proxy 192.168.1.10:8842`) and drop the volumes.
#
# Every value comes from the environment so the image stays generic. See `.env.example`.
{
	# Where Let's Encrypt sends expiry warnings. Caddy asks for a certificate on first run
	# and renews it on its own; there is no certbot step and no cron entry.
	email {$PS_ACME_EMAIL}

	# Where the ACME HTTP-01 challenge is answered, and where the HTTP->HTTPS redirect lives.
	#
	# 80 is the default and needs the NAS to have port 80, which on QTS means moving QTS's own
	# listeners in Control Panel -- the most disruptive step in this whole setup, on a box that
	# is usually doing other jobs for other people.
	#
	# It is also avoidable. A certificate binds to a *name*, and Let's Encrypt only insists on
	# reaching port 80 of the *public address* -- which the Pi holds and relays from. What the Pi
	# forwards to is ours to choose, so the NAS can answer on 8880 and leave QTS alone. The site
	# address below carries its own port for the same reason.
	http_port {$PS_HTTP_PORT:80}

	# Uncomment while testing to avoid Let's Encrypt's rate limits. Certificates issued from
	# staging are NOT publicly trusted, so casting will fail until this is commented out
	# again — which is exactly the failure this whole file exists to avoid, so do not leave
	# it on.
	# acme_ca https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory
}

# Which certificate this deployment uses. `PS_TLS` names one of the two snippets below, and the
# compose overlay that starts Caddy sets it: `tls_public` from docker-compose.proxy.yml,
# `tls_lan` from docker-compose.lan.yml.
#
# A switch rather than a comment saying which lines to swap, because the file is mounted
# read-only out of the repository: editing it to run on a LAN meant carrying that edit forever
# and colliding with it the day a public certificate was wanted.

# Let's Encrypt, for a deployment with a public name (§12).
#
# HTTP-01 only, deliberately.
#
# Caddy would otherwise also try TLS-ALPN-01, which validates on port 443 — and in this
# deployment port 443 of the public address belongs to the Pi, so that attempt cannot succeed.
# Left enabled it is not fatal (Caddy falls back to HTTP-01), but every issuance spends a failed
# validation first, and Let's Encrypt allows only five of those per hostname per hour. Renewal
# is a thing that happens unattended every sixty days; it should not have a wasted failure built
# into it.
#
# Naming the issuer explicitly gives up Caddy's ZeroSSL fallback. That is the trade: one
# predictable path rather than two, for something nobody will be watching when it runs.
(tls_public) {
	tls {
		issuer acme {
			disable_tlsalpn_challenge
		}
	}
}

# Caddy's own CA, for a LAN with no public name.
#
# This is the whole of what makes HTTP/2 available without a domain, and HTTP/2 is what stops a
# grid page queueing sixty covers onto six connections (§20). No ACME, so no port 80, no
# validation and nothing to renew from outside.
#
# The cost is that nothing trusts the certificate until told to. Install the root from
# `/data/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt` on each machine that browses the library.
#
# **Cast receivers cannot be told.** A Chromecast or Android TV fetches media itself, with its
# own TLS stack and no way to add a CA, so casting fails with a black screen while this is on
# (§11). That is the one thing the public path buys that this one cannot.
(tls_lan) {
	tls internal
}

{$PS_DOMAIN} {
	# The site address. `PS_DOMAIN` carries a port when the front door is not on 443, which is
	# how the LAN overlay stays clear of the ports QTS has already taken.
	#
	# On the public path a bare domain here is the entire TLS mechanism: Caddy obtains and renews
	# a Let's Encrypt certificate by itself. The validator reaches it through a relay on the
	# Pi, which holds port 80 of the public address and forwards only the challenge path here.
	# See README.md — "Getting a certificate".
	#
	# HTTP-01 only, deliberately.
	#
	# Caddy would otherwise also try TLS-ALPN-01, which validates on port 443 — and in this
	# deployment port 443 of the public address belongs to the Pi, so that attempt cannot
	# succeed. Left enabled it is not fatal (Caddy falls back to HTTP-01), but every issuance
	# spends a failed validation first, and Let's Encrypt allows only five of those per
	# hostname per hour. Renewal is a thing that happens unattended every sixty days; it
	# should not have a wasted failure built into it.
	#
	# Naming the issuer explicitly gives up Caddy's ZeroSSL fallback. That is the trade: one
	# predictable path rather than two, for something nobody will be watching when it runs.
	import {$PS_TLS}

	reverse_proxy {$PS_UPSTREAM} {
		# The app reads `X-Forwarded-For` to identify a caller for rate limiting, and only
		# believes it when `PRIVATE_STOCK_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS` says a proxy is in front. This
		# is the other half of that agreement.
		#
		# Set rather than appended, so the header holds exactly one entry — the address Caddy
		# itself saw — and anything the client sent under that name is discarded rather than
		# carried along in front of it.
		#
		# **Caddy warns that this line is unnecessary on every start. It is not.** The warning
		# means the default already forwards the header, which is true and beside the point: the
		# default *appends*, so a client that sends one gets its value kept in front of the real
		# address. Measured against this exact file -- a request carrying
		# `X-Forwarded-For: 6.6.6.6` arrives upstream as the peer address alone, and without this
		# line it would arrive as `6.6.6.6, <peer>`. Do not delete it because Caddy said so.
		#
		# If you ever put something else in front of Caddy, this line has to change as well as
		# the hop count, or the app will see a one-entry chain where it expects two and
		# identify nobody. That is the safe direction to get it wrong in.
		header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}

		# The live-updates change feed (§13b) is server-sent events, which a buffering proxy
		# turns into a connection that delivers nothing until it closes. -1 flushes
		# immediately. Harmless for ordinary responses.
		flush_interval -1

		# Idle upstream connections are left at Caddy's default of 2 minutes on purpose: the
		# app's keep-alive (`KEEP_ALIVE_TIMEOUT` in entrypoint.sh) is longer, so Caddy is always
		# the side that closes one. The other way round, a request sent on a connection the app
		# was closing came back as a 502 (§20). Raising this means raising that too.
	}

	# Text only, stated explicitly rather than left to Caddy's default response matcher.
	#
	# Compressing video, WebP or a sprite sheet spends CPU to make the bytes very slightly
	# larger — they are already compressed — on a box whose CPU is the binding constraint
	# (§17), and they are the overwhelming majority of what this app serves.
	#
	# `match` is a *response* matcher: `Content-Type` is not known when the request arrives,
	# so the ordinary `@name` request matcher cannot express this.
	encode {
		gzip
		match {
			header Content-Type text/*
			header Content-Type application/json*
			header Content-Type application/javascript*
			header Content-Type image/svg+xml*
		}
	}

	# No `header` block here on purpose.
	#
	# The app sets its own security headers in `next.config.ts`, so that they hold however it
	# is reached — including on the LAN with no proxy at all. Setting them here too is how the
	# sibling Pi apps ended up serving two different `Strict-Transport-Security` lifetimes and
	# an `X-Frame-Options` that says SAMEORIGIN in one header and DENY in another.

	log {
		output file /var/log/caddy/private-stock-access.log {
			roll_size 10mb
			roll_keep 5
		}

		# A signed media URL carries its credential in `?t=` (§12d) -- a day's access to one
		# item, and a cast session makes dozens of these requests. The access log is read by
		# fail2ban and kept for five rolls; it is not somewhere that credential should sit. The
		# parameter stays so a log reader can still tell a token request from a cookie one.
		#
		# `wrap json` explicitly: fail2ban's filter reads the JSON fields, and the default
		# encoder is not guaranteed to be JSON.
		format filter {
			wrap json
			fields {
				request>uri query {
					replace t redacted
				}
			}
		}
	}
}
